Overview
Week 2 fitted linear mixed models. This week is about deciding which one to keep, and then checking that it is defensible: comparing covariance structures, choosing between ML and REML, testing a variance component on the boundary of its parameter space, and reading the diagnostic plots.
By the end of this sheet you should be able to:

select a structure for \(\mathbf{R}_i\) and for \(\mathbf{G}\) using likelihood ratio tests and information criteria;
say when REML should be used instead of ML, and why;
handle a test whose null puts a variance on the boundary;
interpret the fitted model, with confidence intervals; and
check the model’s assumptions, including the two separate normality assumptions, and know what each diagnostic plot can and cannot tell you.


The data
cattle is the growth experiment from week 1: 60 animals randomised to two treatment groups of 30, weighed on the same eleven occasions two weeks apart, giving 660 measurements. The design is balanced, and the data arrive in wide form, one row per animal, with group followed by t1 to t11. The first exercise reshapes them to long form, which is what lme() needs.
